# Line-ending policy.
#
# `scripts/verify.py` hashes the raw BYTES of data/*.jsonl against
# data/checksums.json. Those checksums were computed on Linux (LF). With
# core.autocrlf=true — the default on this Windows checkout — git rewrites the
# working tree to CRLF on checkout, the hashes no longer match, and verify.py
# reports a FAILING `eval sets unmodified` on a corpus nobody touched.
#
# `-text` disables eol conversion entirely for these paths, so every platform
# gets the exact bytes that were checksummed. The alternative — regenerating
# checksums per platform — would make the checksum meaningless as a
# "the eval set did not change" guarantee, which is the whole point of it.
data/*.jsonl -text
data/checksums.json -text
data/split/*.jsonl -text

# Notebooks write these; keep them byte-stable too.
results/*.json -text
results/*.csv -text
